WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.740
A monkey watches a cloud of moving dots.

2
00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:08.300
It must decide which way they are going.

3
00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:10.640
How does it turn noise into a choice?

4
00:00:11.633 --> 00:00:14.713
Some dots move together, and the rest move at random.

5
00:00:14.713 --> 00:00:17.713
The share moving together is called coherence.

6
00:00:18.717 --> 00:00:23.237
The monkey answers with a quick eye movement to one of two targets, whenever it is

7
00:00:23.237 --> 00:00:24.237
ready.

8
00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:27.710
Stronger motion gives faster and more accurate choices.

9
00:00:28.610 --> 00:00:31.150
With no net motion at all, the choices fall to chance.

10
00:00:32.150 --> 00:00:37.790
Meanwhile, averaged over many trials, a neuron in its parietal cortex fires faster

11
00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:40.390
and faster. Is it making the decision?

12
00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:42.430
Hold that question.

13
00:00:43.233 --> 00:00:46.093
Any single moment of the dots tells you very little.

14
00:00:46.933 --> 00:00:49.013
Each glimpse is a weak hint, buried in noise.

15
00:00:50.033 --> 00:00:51.413
So keep a running total.

16
00:00:52.413 --> 00:00:54.633
Count each glimpse toward one direction or the other.

17
00:00:55.667 --> 00:00:58.127
Hints toward the true direction pile up in step.

18
00:00:59.007 --> 00:01:02.527
The random jiggle points every which way, so it partly cancels.

19
00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:04.540
Here is a question.

20
00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:06.780
You watch four times as long.

21
00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:09.820
Is your running total four times as reliable?

22
00:01:15.983 --> 00:01:19.103
No. It is only twice as reliable.

23
00:01:20.216 --> 00:01:22.156
The signal grows in step with time.

24
00:01:23.036 --> 00:01:25.936
The noise grows too, but only as the square root of time.

25
00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:31.670
Assume each glimpse carries the same average signal, with independent noise of the

26
00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:36.130
same spread. Reliability here means signal divided by that spread.

27
00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:39.310
It grows as the square root of the samples added.

28
00:01:40.283 --> 00:01:41.783
That explains the behaviour.

29
00:01:42.903 --> 00:01:47.083
Weak motion gives a small push per glimpse, so the total takes longer to become

30
00:01:47.083 --> 00:01:48.083
trustworthy.

31
00:01:48.666 --> 00:01:51.426
Waiting longer buys accuracy and costs time.

32
00:01:51.946 --> 00:01:55.546
So a brain that adds up evidence needs a rule for when to stop.

33
00:01:56.550 --> 00:01:58.910
The same arithmetic works across neurons.

34
00:01:59.830 --> 00:02:02.590
It helps explain why some effects of attention matter.

35
00:02:03.470 --> 00:02:04.830
The lesson returns to that.

36
00:02:05.733 --> 00:02:10.633
Adding up noisy evidence makes it more reliable, because the signal adds in step

37
00:02:10.633 --> 00:02:12.613
while the noise partly cancels.
